    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q1-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) The median lies in the 20.5 to 25.5 class, which starts at cumulative frequency 21. [M1] The estimate is 20.5 + 5(32 −21)/14 = 24.43 h . [A1] (b) Use midpoints 5.5, 15.5, 23, 28, 35.5, 50. [B1] The totals are fx2 = 42331.75. X f = 64, X fx = 1528.5, X [A1] The grouped mean and population standard deviation are ¯x = 1528.5 r42331.75 −¯x2 ≈9.54 h . ≈23.88 h , s = [M1] [A1] 64 64\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q1-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(c) The mean is below the median, suggesting negative skew: [M1] [A1] (d) The quartile interpolations use Q1 = 10.5 + 10(16 −5)/16 and Q3 = 25.5 + 5(48 −35)/17. Under the source’s two-standard-deviation rule, use the unrounded grouped mean and standard deviation for both fences: Interpolation gives Q1 = 17.375, Q3 ≈29.324. The 1.5-IQR fences are about −0.55 and 47.25. [B1] Only the upper class crosses a fence, so high outliers are possible under this rule: The handwritten solution uses the alternative rule ¯x ± 2s, with fences about 4.80 and 42.97. [M1] Under that rule both outer classes could contain outliers: Grouped data cannot establish which individual values occur: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:660.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
