    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Fill in the diagram: Let p = P(A). [B1] [A1] Then P(A ∩B) = 3 8p [M1] [A1] and P(A′ ∩B) = 15 28(1 −p). [M1] [A1] Alternatively label the Venn regions:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Their respective probabilities for A-only, both, B-only and neither are 5p/8, 3p/8, 15(1 −p)/28, 13(1 −p)/28. The middle regions must be in the ratio 4 : 5. For the source’s simultaneous-equations solution, call these region probabilities x, y, z, w, respectively: The given conditionals yield x + y = 3 y y + z = 4 y z + w = 15 z 8, 9, 28. Cross-multiplying gives 5y = 3x, 5y = 4z, 13z = 15w. Hence y = 3x/5, x = 4z/3. Substituting into x + y + z + w = 1 gives 8x/5 + z + w = 1, then 47z/15 + w = 1. Add 47z = 15 −15w to 13z = 15w to get 60z = 15. Thus z = 1/4, w = 13/60, x = 1/3, y = 1/5, so\" style=\"--madas-width:653.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q8-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"P(A) = x + y = 8/15 . The same calculation can be carried out with p alone: Since P(A | B) = 4/9, the intersection accounts for four ninths of P(B) and the other part five ninths: [M1] [A1] Thus     5 3 8p = 4 15 28(1 −p) , 7p = 8(1 −p), p = 8/15 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 10 marks.</p>"
