    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-v/q9",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"It is an actual fact that “sleeping with your clothes and shoes on is strongly correlated with waking up with a headache”. Evidently the conclusion is that “sleeping with your clothes and shoes on causes a headache”. Discuss the validity of the above conclusion indicating how a strong correlation could be possible in the above scenario. ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"A correlation alone does not establish cause: For example, alcohol consumption could increase the chance of falling asleep without getting changed and independently increase the chance of waking with a headache: [M1] One can therefore observe the association in both groups even when changing whether someone removes their clothes has no effect on the headache risk: The common factor could produce the reported association . [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 9 is 2 marks.</p>"
