    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q12-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:545.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q12-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"With A travelling away from the pulley and B uphill, the two friction magnitudes are (2g)/7 = 2.8 N and (3g)(0.8)/7 = 3.36 N. The downhill component of B’s weight is 3g(0.6) = 17.64 N. (a) The equations are 30 −T −2.8 = 2a [M1] [A1] and T −3.36 −17.64 = 3a. [A1] Eliminating T leaves 30 −2.8 −3.36 −17.64 = 5a, so 6.2 = 5a. Back-substitute in T = 27.2 −2a. Adding gives a = 1.24 m s−2, [A1] consistent with the assumed direction: [A1] Hence T = 24.72 N . [M1] [A1] (b) After 1.5 s, B has moved 2(1.24)(1.5)2 = 1.395 m 1 [B1] [A1] with speed 1.86 m s−1. [M1] [A1] After the string breaks its uphill acceleration is −(3.36 + 17.64)/3 = −7 m s−2. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
