    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-v/q15",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q15-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A man is trying to push a box of weight W up the line of greatest slope of a rough plane inclined at an angle α to the horizontal, by applying a force parallel to a line of greatest slope. The coefficient of friction between the man’s feet and the plane is µ , µ &gt; tan α . The coefficient of friction between the box and the plane is µ′. The man and the box are both modelled as particles and air resistance is ignored. Show, with detailed workings, that if the man is to succeed in pushing the box up this plane his weight must exceed  µ ′ + tan α  W   . ( 10 )  µ − tan α \" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q15-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q15-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Starting with a diagram for each: Let P be the man’s pushing force and K his weight: [B1] [A1] To move the box uphill, P must exceed the combined downslope weight component and limiting friction, W(sin α + µ′ cos α). [M1] [A1] The box exerts an equal force on the man down the plane: [M1] [A1] The maximum upslope friction at his feet is µK cos α, and it must cover both P and K sin α. [A1] Thus\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-v/q15-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"K(µ cos α −sin α) ≥P &gt; W(µ′ cos α + sin α). [M1] [A1] Divide the limiting inequality by cos α &gt; 0 to obtain K(µ −tan α) &gt; W(µ′ + tan α). The requested bound is K &gt; W(µ′ + tan α)/(µ −tan α) . Since µ &gt; tan α, division by the positive coefficient gives the stated strict inequality: [B1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 15 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
